> What'd be the easiest way to reproduce the problem you're seeing?

I suggest to reproduce the problem with a null_blk device. The attached scr=
ipt
"setup_dmz_nullb" prepares the null_blk device with desired conditions, set=
 up
dm-zoned device on top of it and prepares xfs volume at the path /mnt. The
script uses dmzadm command in dm-zoned-tools [1]. (If you use Fedora, its
package is avilable).

[1] https://github.com/westerndigitalcorporation/dm-zoned-tools

After the set up, please run the fio command below. It keeps on rewriting f=
iles
on the dm-zoned device, and creates a number of works for the dmz_cwq workq=
ueue.

# fio --name=3Djob --numjobs=3D64 --rw=3Dwrite --ioengine=3Dlibaio --iodept=
h=3D128 --nrfiles=3D32 --filesize=3D1M --group_reporting=3D1 --directory=3D=
/mnt --loops=3D128

Strictly speaking, this fio workload is not the problematic long xfs unmoun=
t.
However, during this file creation, workqueue monitor shows different numbe=
r
of in-flight works depending on the kernel. I think and hope this inf-light
works number difference is good enough for you to debug further.

